 Which part of an arrangement can change while its pieces remain?
 
+<!-- @hardware_entropy_decay -->
+
+Along the wall on your right as you come in, a rig holds three shapes that weather together: a box, a sphere and a cylinder, with the same wear pushed to all three at once. The rust rises slowly by itself and faster while you move. Swing your hands, squeeze a grip, turn your head. Scratches follow your hands and fade when you stop; the grime and the rust stay. Nothing in the rig draws a random number. In the headset, a surface can look worn by accident when its grime and rust are a record of how you moved in front of it.
+
+<!-- @ -->
+
 <!-- @random_decay_multimesh -->
 
-Watch the two stacks long enough for their changes to begin. Choose one piece and compare its orientation with the line of its neighbours. Then look at its position and colour separately. Use TOGGLE to pause the process and inspect the difference; RESET restores the starting arrangement.
+Further in, a long display crosses the hall. On your right as you face it stand twenty cubes in a block five wide and four high; on your left, a hundred and twenty prisms in twelve columns of ten. Every piece is the same size. None of them has a body, and the way on passes through the prisms.
+
+The pieces may already be turning when you arrive; after a **RESET** they stand in line for a few seconds before they begin. Each turns in the plane of its stack, a little at a time. Nothing else changes. Their size never changes, and in this room neither does their place or their colour. An arrangement can become less regular without any piece moving, shrinking, disappearing or becoming a different kind of object.
 
-The changes do not happen uniformly across the whole display. Pieces acquire different amounts of drift, rotation and darkening. Yet their size stays fixed in this version. An arrangement can become less regular without every object shrinking, disappearing or becoming a different kind of object.
+The demonstration keeps a changing state for each instance. Every piece carries one hidden number that starts at zero and grows by itself, and now and then a random kick lands on a piece and adds to it. The larger the number, the harder that piece is shaken. Its angle has no limit: each small turn is added to the last. A small angular change repeated many times can produce a visibly altered orientation even though no single step seems dramatic. Give it minutes. Half a minute after a RESET the average piece has turned by about a degree, after five minutes by several, and exactly how many depends on the machine drawing the room.
 
-Pause while a chosen piece remains easy to recognise. Compare its edges with the original stack direction, then compare its centre with neighbouring centres. A piece can turn substantially while moving only a little, so one broad description of disorder can conceal different changes. Resume briefly and pause again, keeping the same piece in mind. Check its colour separately from those spatial comparisons. If it becomes difficult to identify, RESET gives you the ordered arrangement again. The renewed view restores a reference for comparison; it need not promise an identical sequence of later perturbations. Try explaining one changed relation before judging the whole stack's appearance.
+After a minute or two, find the piece that has turned furthest and lay its edge against the line of its neighbours. It has not moved, so its neighbours are still its neighbours. Then find one that has barely turned, and explain that one difference before judging the whole stack.
 
-The demonstration keeps a changing state for each instance. Random perturbations contribute to its movement, while bounds, damping and a gradually increasing decay value constrain what those perturbations can do. The result accumulates over time. A small angular change repeated many times can produce a visibly altered orientation even though no single step seems dramatic.
+Left to its defaults, the hidden number also makes each piece drift, sink and darken, so one broad description of disorder can conceal different changes. Here the drift, the sinking and the darkening are switched off, and only turning is left to follow.
+
+Both stacks now run at the same rate. Each piece in either stack follows the same rule: a stack receives kicks in proportion to its number of pieces, so every piece can expect the same share. At its defaults the display runs the cubes three times as fast as the prisms, and the cubes turn several times as far. That difference is mostly the setting, not the shape. What still differs here is shape, colour, number and spacing, so a difference you think you see is not yet a difference of shape alone.
+
+The panel in front of the display is labelled DECAY. The label names the model's presentation; it is not a measurement of thermodynamic entropy or a complete physical explanation of how a material deteriorates. **TOGGLE** pauses the turning and keeps the time already run; press it again and the turning resumes where it stopped. **RESET** puts every piece back in line and starts the delay over. The renewed view restores a reference for comparison, not the turning that followed it: RESET does not rewind the random draws, so the turns that come after it are new. Note which piece leads before a RESET; a minute or two after it, a different piece will usually lead.
+
